@lang en

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- The Interpretation Act 1978 (1978 c. 30), section 7: references to service by post.
--
-- Source: the converter-made plain-text rendering in ../../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-1978-30.txt, not the official text (see SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE ANSWERS
--   * when section 7 applies to a document an Act says is to be served by post;
--   * whether posting a letter is deemed to be service;
--   * the time at which service is deemed to be effected, given the time at which the letter would be delivered in the
--     ordinary course of post.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE REFUSES, because the text is silent
--   * how long the ordinary course of post takes: the Act has no figure and no table, so the caller supplies the time;
--   * the time of service once "the contrary is proved": the section says the deemed time yields, and not what time then governs.
--
-- Whether section 7 reaches an enactment at all (the Act, and subordinate legislation, made at various dates) is answered by
-- uk-interpretation-application.l4. This module answers what the section says once it applies.

§ `Section 7: references to service by post`

-- A time of day is a day and a clock reading. No zone: the section names none, and section 9 is a rule for the times an Act
-- "refers to", which this is not (fork F6 in NOTES.md).
DECLARE `A point in time` HAS
    `the day`    IS A DATE
    `the hour`   IS A NUMBER
    `the minute` IS A NUMBER

DECLARE `A letter served by post` HAS
    `an Act authorises or requires the document to be served by post` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the contrary intention appears`                                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `the letter is properly addressed`                                IS A BOOLEAN
    `the letter is pre-paid`                                          IS A BOOLEAN
    `the letter is posted`                                            IS A BOOLEAN
    `the letter contains the document`                                IS A BOOLEAN
    `the contrary is proved as to the time of service`                IS A BOOLEAN
    `when the letter would be delivered in the ordinary course of post` IS A MAYBE `A point in time`

-- A refusal is an expression at any type; each name is its message.

-- src:25 | Where an Act authorises or requires any document to be served by post (whether the expression “serve” or the expression “give” or “send” or any other expression is used) then, unless the contrary intention appears, the service is deemed to be effected by properly addressing, pre-paying and posting a letter containing the document and, unless the contrary is proved, to have been effected at the time at which the letter would be delivered in the ordinary course of post.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 7 (src:25): the section does not say how long the ordinary course of post takes
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the Interpretation Act 1978 does not say how long the ordinary course of post takes, so the caller must supply when the letter would be delivered` MEANS
    REFUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 does not say how long the ordinary course of post takes, so the caller must supply when the letter would be delivered"

@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 7 (src:25): "unless the contrary is proved"; the section does not say what time governs once it is
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 7 says the deemed time yields to proof of the contrary and does not say what time then governs` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 7 says the deemed time yields to proof of the contrary and does not say what time then governs"

@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 7 (src:25): the deemed time exists only where service is deemed to be effected by posting
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 7 does not deem service to be effected by this letter, so it gives no time of service` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 7 does not deem service to be effected by this letter, so it gives no time of service"

-- "Where an Act authorises or requires any document to be served by post ..., then, unless the contrary intention appears".
-- FALSE here means that the section does not apply, not that service by post is forbidden.
-- src:24 | 7 References to service by post.
-- src:25 | Where an Act authorises or requires any document to be served by post (whether the expression “serve” or the expression “give” or “send” or any other expression is used) then, unless the contrary intention appears, the service is deemed to be effected by properly addressing, pre-paying and posting a letter containing the document and, unless the contrary is proved, to have been effected at the time at which the letter would be delivered in the ordinary course of post.

@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 7 (src:24-25)
GIVEN letter IS A `A letter served by post`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 7 applies to` letter MEANS
        letter's `an Act authorises or requires the document to be served by post`
    AND NOT letter's `the contrary intention appears`

-- "the service is deemed to be effected by properly addressing, pre-paying and posting a letter containing the document".
-- FALSE means that section 7 does not deem service to be effected by this letter; it does not say service did not happen.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 7 (src:24-25)
GIVEN letter IS A `A letter served by post`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 7 deems service to be effected by posting` letter MEANS
        `section 7 applies to` letter
    AND letter's `the letter is properly addressed`
    AND letter's `the letter is pre-paid`
    AND letter's `the letter is posted`
    AND letter's `the letter contains the document`

-- "and, unless the contrary is proved, to have been effected at the time at which the letter would be delivered in the ordinary course of post".
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 7 (src:24-25)
GIVEN letter IS A `A letter served by post`
GIVETH A `A point in time`
`section 7 — the time at which service is deemed to be effected` letter MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `section 7 deems service to be effected by posting` letter
            THEN `section 7 does not deem service to be effected by this letter, so it gives no time of service`
        IF letter's `the contrary is proved as to the time of service`
            THEN `section 7 says the deemed time yields to proof of the contrary and does not say what time then governs`
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER letter's `when the letter would be delivered in the ordinary course of post`
            WHEN JUST t THEN t
            WHEN NOTHING THEN `the Interpretation Act 1978 does not say how long the ordinary course of post takes, so the caller must supply when the letter would be delivered`
